{"name":"Funky Frog","hex":"#98bd3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"funky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Offbeat, unconventional or eccentric.","Not quite right; of questionable quality; not appropriate to the context."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/funky"},{"term":"frog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a class of small tailless amphibians of the order Anura that typically hop.","A member of this class that is not a toad."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt or trap frogs.","To use a pronged plater to transfer (cells) to another plate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Funk","blocks":[{"text":"Funk is a music genre that originated in African-American communities in the mid-1960s when musicians created a rhythmic, danceable new form of music through a mixture of various music genres that were popular among African-Americans in the mid-20th century. It deemphasizes melody and chord progressions and focuses on a strong rhythmic groove of a bassline played by an electric bassist and a drum part played by a percussionist, often at slower tempos than other popular music. Funk typically consists of a complex percussive groove with rhythm instruments playing interlocking grooves that create a \"hypnotic\" and \"danceable\" feel. Early funk, specifically James Brown, fused jazz and blues, and added a syncopated drum groove."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/James-Brown_1973.jpg/330px-James-Brown_1973.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funk","wikidata":"Q164444"},{"title":"Frog","blocks":[{"text":"A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura. Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be called toads, but the distinction between frogs and toads is informal and purely cosmetic, and does not reflect taxonomy or evolutionary history."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_%2849661076226%29.jpg/330px-Red-eyed_Leaf_Frog_%2849661076226%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frog","wikidata":"Q53636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/500px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/1280px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Meters_Wikepedia.jpg/500px-Meters_Wikepedia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The rhythm section of a funk band—the electric bass, drums, electric guitar and keyboards—is the heartbeat of the funk sound. Pictured here are the Meters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeters_Wikepedia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Funk.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Funk.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Funk.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Funk.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Simple kick and snare funk motif. The kick first sounds two onbeats, which are then answered by two offbeats. The snare sounds the backbeat.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFunk.tiff"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png/500px-Thirteenth_chord_collapsed.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"ht